<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ban Mueang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Pho Khun Ban Mueang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ban Mueang was the second king of Sukhothai, the elder son of its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-inthrathit">Si Inthrathit</a>. According to the inscription attributed to his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a>, he succeeded his father and ruled while Ram Khamhaeng served as a commander. He was succeeded by Ram Khamhaeng, probably around 1279, and little else is known of his reign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ban-mueang.jpg" alt="Ban Mueang" width="320" height="567" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ananda · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phra_Ruang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ban Mueang was a son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-inthrathit">Si Inthrathit</a> of Sukhothai and his wife, Lady Sueang, and belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phra-ruang-dynasty">Phra Ruang dynasty</a>. His name means one who delights the nation. In Thai it is often misspelt with a different letter that is pronounced the same and changes the meaning to one who protects the nation. In the Pu Khun Chit Khun Chot Inscription, Inscription No. 45, dated 1392/93 CE, he is called simply Ban.</p><p>The Ram Khamhaeng Inscription, Inscription No. 1, said to have been made in 1292/93 CE, contains the autobiography of his younger brother Ram Khamhaeng. It names the parents and Ban Mueang, says that five children were born to them, three boys and two girls, and that the eldest brother died as a child.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ban Mueang succeeded his father in a year that is not known and reigned until his death. The inscription has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a> say that in his father's lifetime he brought him the game, fish, fruit and elephants he caught and the captives, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold he took in raids, and that when his father died he served his elder brother as steadfastly as he had served his father. When his elder brother died, he says, he received the whole kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Both inscriptions state that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng">Ram Khamhaeng</a> succeeded Ban Mueang on his death, which took place around 1279. The Pu Khun Chit Khun Chot Inscription also places him in the lineage of the House of Phra Ruang. The chronicle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinakalamali">Jinakalamali</a> records that Ban Mueang had a son, Ngua Nam Thum, who later became king of Sukhothai. Following the old custom of naming a child after an ancestor, a great-great-grandchild was named after him and came to the throne as Mahathammaracha IV.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ban Mueang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to the inscription of his son Ram Khamhaeng, Si Inthrathit's family fought neighbouring rulers to secure the new kingdom, and his elder son <b>Ban Mueang</b> succeeded him before Ram Khamhaeng took the throne, probably around 1279.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai Kingdom · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sukhothai, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. Died in Sukhothai, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17,99.82&amp;year=1264" aria-label="Sukhothai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7265 1532 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ban Mueang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7715 1802h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7715 1802h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17,99.82&amp;year=1264">What was here in 1264? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
